@page "/docs/common/data"

@* Data and filters: the request a data component hands its provider and the result it gets back,
   loading from EF Core, fields, filters in code, the operators, text and case, and dates. Calling
   an API, the filter syntax, OData and the server's limits are on Data from an API (DataApi.razor).
   From the data foundations design (artifacts/izzyblazor-data-foundations-design.md) and Core/Data. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="data">
    <Lead>
        A <Code>Table</Code> or <Code>DataGrid</Code> with an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> asks it for rows with a
        <Code>DataRequest</Code>, and the provider answers with a <Code>DataResult</Code>. The request contains the range,
        the sort and the filter. It applies itself to an EF Core query, so the database does the work. A filter's values
        never become code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The request and the result</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The component calls its <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> each time it needs rows. The <Code>DataRequest</Code> it
            passes describes those rows:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Skip</code>, <code>Take</code></td><td>The range: how many rows to skip, and how many to take (null for every row). <code>Page</code> gives the same range as a page number, from 1.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Sort</code></td><td>A list of <code>SortField</code>s. Each has a key and whether it's descending. A Table sorts by one column, and a DataGrid can sort by several.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Filter</code></td><td>The filter, or null. A Table's filter is a <code>Filter.All</code> with one condition for each filtered column, plus any other conditions from a bound <code>Filter</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Search</code></td><td>Free text to look for in the searchable fields. A Table doesn't set it. A DataGrid with <code>Searchable</code> sets it from its search box. To search from your own search box, set it in the provider: <code>request with { Search = query }</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Parent</code>, <code>FilterBranch</code></td><td>For a TreeList, the row whose children to load, or null for the top rows. Answer it with <code>ToTreeResultAsync</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Cursor</code></td><td>When the component pages by cursor, the <code>Next</code> or <code>Previous</code> cursor from an earlier result.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NeedsTotal</code></td><td>Whether the component needs the total count. It's false when the component pages by cursor, where the total is optional.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>CancellationToken</code></td><td>Canceled when a newer request replaces this one. Pass it on to your queries.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            The <Code>DataResult</Code> contains the rows. Add the <Code>Total</Code> for numbered pages, or the
            <Code>Next</Code> and <Code>Previous</Code> cursors when the component pages by cursor:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Result</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">From a database</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The simplest way needs no provider. Pass an EF Core query as a Table's <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts,
            filters and pages it. Write an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> when you need more. For example, a provider can limit
            the rows to the signed-in user on each request, call an API, or run your own search.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In a provider, <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> does the work. It filters, searches and sorts the query. It counts the
            matching rows when <Code>NeedsTotal</Code> is true, and returns the rows in the range. You pass it EF Core's
            async methods, so IzzyBlazor doesn't depend on EF Core:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" HighlightLines="7-9">@FromDatabase</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The request contains the component's columns, and each column defines how its key reads an item. So the provider
            never maps keys to properties. Each condition becomes a LINQ expression on the column's own property, and its
            value becomes a query parameter.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>For a list in memory, <Code>request.ToResult(list.AsQueryable())</Code> does the same.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>To filter, sort and take the range without counting, call <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code>.
                <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> and <Code>ApplySorting</Code> do one part each.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                When the provider throws, the Table logs the exception and shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#when-loading-fails">When loading fails</Anchor>. A request that
                the Table canceled isn't an error.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A field is a value the data can be sorted, filtered or searched by. Each field has a key and an expression that
            reads its value. It also sets what it allows: sorting, filtering, search, and which operators. A Table's columns
            are its fields.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>PropertyColumn</Code>'s key is its property path in lower case (<Code>customer.name</Code>), unless it
            has a <Code>SortKey</Code>. A <Code>TemplateColumn</Code>'s key is its <Code>SortKey</Code>. It filters and
            searches by its <Code>FilterBy</Code> and sorts by its <Code>SortBy</Code>. A <Code>DynamicColumn</Code>'s key is
            its <Code>Field</Code> in lower case, with each run of characters other than letters and digits replaced by an
            underscore (<Code>Q1 2026</Code> is <Code>q1_2026</Code>), unless it has a <Code>SortKey</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To get fields where there are no columns, such as on a server, call <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code>. It
            returns a field for each public top-level property that a filter can compare. Each field's key is the property
            name in lower case.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Every field is sortable, filterable, groupable and aggregatable. Text fields are also searchable, and an enum
            field's choices are the enum's names.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The caption comes from <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The format comes from <Code>[DisplayFormat]</Code> and <Code>[DataType]</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Properties with <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code> or <Code>[Display(AutoGenerateField = false)]</Code> are left out.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>To choose the fields and what each allows, declare them yourself:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Declared</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To limit the operators a field allows, set its <Code>Operators</Code>. On a column, set
            <Code>FilterOperators</Code>: the first is the default, and a condition with any other matches nothing.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To filter a computed value, or a value stored differently from how it's shown, set the field's own
            <Code>Filter</Code> and build the condition in your code. It receives the condition, with its values parsed for
            the field's type. It returns the filter on the item, or null to use the usual one. A Table column has the same
            <Code>Filter</Code> parameter:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Custom</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>It runs for every operator, so check the operator you handle.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>It matches case, unless you lower-case both sides.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters in code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A filter is a tree. A <Code>FieldFilter</Code> is a condition on one field. A <Code>FilterGroup</Code> joins
            conditions with and or or. A <Code>NotFilter</Code> matches what its filter doesn't. Each condition names its
            field by key, and the Table's URL uses the same keys: <Code>?f-status=</Code> filters the field
            <Code>status</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@InCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To set a Table's filter from C#, bind its <Code>Filter</Code> on
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor>. With an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>, the
            provider receives the whole filter as <Code>request.Filter</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Bound</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            When a column's filter can show a condition, the condition appears in that filter. By default, a Table's columns
            show these conditions:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Column type</th><th>A Table column's default filter</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Text</td><td>Contains</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Number</td><td>Between, at least, at most or equals</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Date</td><td>On day</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Choice</td><td>In</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            A DataGrid's columns, and a Table column with <Code>FilterOperators</Code>, let the user choose an operator above
            the value. So they show any condition that uses one of their operators. A column with <Code>FilterValues</Code>
            or <Code>FilterList</Code> shows In as a list of checkboxes. Any other condition, such as an or group, still
            applies. It appears as a tag the user can remove.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To pass a condition on to an API, read it with <Code>Find</Code>. It returns the condition on that field that
            every matching row must meet, or null:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Find</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Operators</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            An operator sets how a condition compares the field's value. The operators a field allows depend on its type. An
            operator the type doesn't allow matches nothing.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Types</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Equals</code></td><td>All</td><td>The value. With a null value, the same as <code>IsNull</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NotEquals</code></td><td>All</td><td>Anything but the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>In</code></td><td>All</td><td>Any of the values in a list.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NotIn</code></td><td>All</td><td>None of the values in a list.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>LessThan</code>, <code>LessThanOrEqual</code>, <code>GreaterThan</code>, <code>GreaterThanOrEqual</code></td><td>Numbers, dates, times</td><td>Values below or above the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Between</code></td><td>Numbers, dates, times</td><td>Values from the first to the second, both included. A null end has no limit.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>OnDay</code></td><td>Dates</td><td>Dates on the day in the value. See <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#dates">Dates</Anchor>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Contains</code>, <code>DoesNotContain</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Text that contains the value, or doesn't. <code>DoesNotContain</code> also matches null.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>StartsWith</code>, <code>EndsWith</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Text that starts or ends with the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IsEmpty</code>, <code>IsNotEmpty</code></td><td>Text</td><td><code>IsEmpty</code>: empty text or null. <code>IsNotEmpty</code>: text with at least one character.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IsNull</code>, <code>IsNotNull</code></td><td>Any type that can be null</td><td><code>IsNull</code>: no value. <code>IsNotNull</code>: any value.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            "All" means text, numbers, dates, times, enums, booleans and Guids. Enums, booleans and Guids allow only the
            operators marked All, plus the null checks when they can be null.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Values are parsed for the field's type, so <Code>"10"</Code> filters a number and <Code>"Paid"</Code> filters an
            enum. A value that doesn't parse matches nothing, and so does a condition on a field that doesn't exist.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                Numbers are parsed first in the current culture's format (<Code>"1.234,5"</Code> in German), then in the
                invariant format (<Code>"1234.5"</Code>).
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Dates and times are parsed first in ISO format (<Code>"2026-10-28"</Code>), then in the current culture's
                format.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                On a whole-number field, a fractional bound rounds in the direction of the comparison. "At least 2.5" matches 3
                or more, and "at most 2.5" matches 2 or less.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>A bound outside the range of the field's type matches every row or none.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Text and case</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Text filters ignore case by default, as a Table's filters do. To ignore case, IzzyBlazor lower-cases both sides
            of the comparison, so a database index can't be used. Two settings change this: one for a single condition, and
            one for every condition on a field.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Applies to</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>MatchCase</code> on a <code>FieldFilter</code></td><td>That condition</td><td>Matches upper and lower case exactly. For <code>Equals</code>, it matches spaces exactly too.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>FilterCase</code> on a Table or DataGrid</td><td>Every column filter and the search</td><td>Sets how text is compared, with the values below. The columns pass it on to their fields.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Case</code> on a <code>DataField</code></td><td>Every condition on that field</td><td>The same values, for fields you declare.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th><code>FilterCase</code></th><th>How text compares</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>IgnoreCase</code> (the default)</td><td>Upper and lower case are the same. Both sides are lower-cased, so an index can't be used.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>MatchCase</code></td><td>Text is compared as it is. In memory, the comparison is exact. In a database, the database's own comparison still applies. For example, EF Core uses <code>LIKE</code> for starts-with and ends-with, and SQLite's <code>LIKE</code> ignores ASCII case.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Database</code></td><td>Text is compared as it is, using the database's collation, so an index can be used. On a list in memory, it's the same as <code>MatchCase</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            A comparison ignores case only when both settings allow it. A condition with <Code>MatchCase</Code> compares
            text as it is, whatever the field's <Code>Case</Code>. A field whose <Code>Case</Code> isn't
            <Code>IgnoreCase</Code> compares text as it is, even when the condition doesn't set <Code>MatchCase</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The filter's text form doesn't contain either setting. So an endpoint that reads the filter, such as an export,
            sets <Code>Case</Code> on its own fields to match the page. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api#filters-as-text">Filters as text</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Dates</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnDay</Code> matches every date on one day. How it compares depends on the field's type:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field type</th><th>How <code>OnDay</code> compares</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>DateOnly</code></td><td>The date equals the day.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DateTime</code></td><td>The value falls between the start of the day and the start of the next day, so an index can be used.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DateTimeOffset</code></td><td>The value's local date, in the offset it was recorded with, equals the day. EF Core can't translate this on SQLite.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            When a field has a time and the condition's value is only a date, the condition covers the whole day, not just
            midnight. <Code>Equals</Code> with a date matches any time on that day, and "on or before 3 Jan" includes 3 Jan at
            noon.
        </TextBlock>

